As I understand it, all the interest rates would tend to be equal
because lending would tend to flow from the lower interest combinations
to the higher interest ones.

Certainly, contracts could be denominated in anything, though I doubt
that "labor" is well-enough defined to be useful. However, barter
has awfully high transaction costs--in fact I assume that the
reason people suffering from hyperinflation don't simply switch
to barter is that the transaction costs are too high. I've wondered
if computers would be able to lower the transaction costs of barter
so far that it would supplant currency.

This reminds me of something I also wonder about....I like the
idea of cyphercurrency, but I'm not sure how it can be backed
with anything. (And if the governments fall, we won't be using
their currencies.) Is there some way to connect the money to
a physical object (like a gram of gold) without losing anonymity?
How well can an economy work if there's a chance that anything
you use to back the currency might suddenly become a great deal
more common? (For instance, a gold-backed currency becomes
relatively worthless if someone discovers a cheap way to refine
gold from sea water.)

Nancy Lebovitz


