From: Mr John M Casey <jmcasey@HK.Super.NET>
Date: Tue, 10 May 1994 08:24:40 +0800 (HKT)
Subject: [#94-5-122] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

>  It is probably true that real interest rates haven't risen over the past
> few centuries (the uncertanties in how to adjust for inflation and risk of
> default limit the usefullness of this comparison). Interest rates appear
> to have some relation to the growth rate now. I am not sure whether that
> was true before the industrial revolution.

Actually, in theory real rates are a function of people's fears for
preserving their capital. Thus real rates will tend to be highest in
periods of recession, or any time when they are worried that their
currency is depreciating. Note this also can mean times when people are
expecting inflation to rise, but it hasn't yet, say at the beginning of an
upturn.

On the history of interest rates, New Amsterdam interest rates were around
7-8% a year. They remained at that level throughout the Industrial
Revolution in the US, and then started coming down towards the end of the
19th century. They rose again with WWI and stayed quite high (over 10%) in
the Roaring '20s, before collapsing with stocks in the '30s. 

>  I think that so far the value of capital has been limited by the need for
> labor to make that capital useful. I think that sometime in the next
> century that will change to the point where the main limit on the value
> of capital will be physical limits on the speed at which resources get
> moved around, and that this transition will control the increase in rates.

Hear, hear. People always forget to factor in the changes that inevitably
take place when things start to become intolerable. They draw straight
line extrapolations, which is of extremely low value. Have you seen the
book Unlimited Wealth? Not terrific, but a good exposition on the nature
of information as capital.





