I don't agree that any restrictions on voting should be enabled.  I don't
think it captures the openness of the system I envision.

> Am I saying that your posts are not worth reading because I hate you, or
> because I genuinely believe it to be true?
If you are, someone could vote on your vote, saying that your vote is not
valid for this person.  You use your mind to decide which entities you trust,
just like now.  If one entity shows you good judgement, and offers to sell
you more judgement for a small fee, you might be inclined to do so.

I have a proposal for doing this on a usenet goup.  You have a moderator
for group.x.meta, which comments on goup.x.  For each post on group.x
there is created a faq type post, which is automaticaly canceled and
reposted with updates, which consists of a subject line of the post being
commented on, and a list of the votes such as:

Type_of_vote;identifier_of_voter;SHS_hash_of_vote 

You can view this to see that your vote was correctly registered, and the
moderation site could store a list of headers for the votes, and nuke the
body except for the hash value.  You can also search through the meta-post
for the people/services you trust.  The type of vote could be a changing
value that requires purchased information to interprit, allowing voters to
charge for their service.

The votes could go out on any sort of mailing list that the moderator was
on, and could be pre-filtered and sent out to subscribers by different
brokers, or the brokers could run the voting list.

This could be a way of moderating without a fixed moderator, by ageing
articles faster when they don't draw favorable votes.

You would also be able to nuke adds this way, and I am sure this service
would be provided for free by some of the more anti-capatilists out there:-)

