Subject: [#94-5-124] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

>   I mean human labor is never exchanged for things owned by robots (or owned
> by humans who own robots) due to lack of demand for human labor by entities
> with access to robotic labor. The question "to whom does X have value?" is

There's a book by Roger Penrose called The Emperor's New Mind, that
discusses in painstaking detail the possibility of computers (incl robots)
ever being able to truly make the leaps of logic that can enable a human
to, for instance, prove or disprove a theorem. Turing Machines et al not
in certain cases knowing when to shut themselves off, for instance. Okay,
I know genetic programming addresses some of these issues by allowing
learning, mutation and even improvisation, but ultimately there will be
some process known by the creator (or creator of the creator, on to
infinite regress) that the robot will not and can not know. And this, I
believe, will always be the capital that humans can trade for.

Does this create a Blade Runner society, with countless poor and a few
elite who hold the secrets? Maybe. It may also, if the human inventors are
smart enough and, as a group, disciplined enough, allow for failsafe
programming that alters the robotic utility function so that
human-produced goods and services are more highly valued than
robot-produced goods. This happens all the time in human society: why
would anyone *ever* buy a Mercedes when there are high-end Toyota's that
are, let's face it, every bit as good at half the price? Answer: because
the Mercedes has a different utility function.

You'd also have to make sure that robots were largely ignorant of their
utility function (just as humans are), so that robots able to write
programs couldn't or wouldn't hack the utility functions of other robots.



